\paragraph{Beyond manipulation.}
We also evaluate \name{} on the ExoRL RND datasets (\cref{tab:benchmarks}).
\name{} attains the highest mean return ($700.5$ vs. $648.2$ for FB), leading on quadruped and point-mass, while FB remains stronger on walker ($-7.6\%$) and cheetah ($-19.7\%$). 
This split is consistent with our analysis: the DMC tasks are dense-reward locomotion problems without interaction bottlenecks, so the failure mode Density-FB targets is largely absent there, and changing the objective brings no systematic advantage.

\section{Conclusion}
\aditya{Update}
% We studied a failure mode of SM approximations in which learned representations lose distinctions between actions at control bottlenecks. 
We introduced \name{}, which treats FB's bilinear output as a score, normalizes it into a distribution over future states with a softmax, learns this distribution through a categorical Bellman objective, and evaluates actions by integrating rewards under it. We traced FB's manipulation failures to control bottlenecks, proved that rank-limited squared fitting can erase the corresponding action distinctions while softmax fitting preserves them on the same instance, and showed that the same distinction governs behavior across three levels of evidence: didactic MDPs with known solutions, critic diagnostics during cube manipulation, and downstream success on eleven manipulation tasks in OGBench, MetaWorld, and ManiSkill, where Density-FB outperforms FB on every task.

